 1 big thing: Anti-Trumpism fuels youth vote 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Harvard Youth Poll , Fall 2026; Chart: Emily Peck/Axios Nearly 2 in 3 U.S. adults younger than 30 who plan to vote in the upcoming midterms say their main motivation is to oppose President Trump and his policies, Emily Peck and Margaret Talev report from a new Harvard Youth Poll. 

 77% of respondents disapprove of the president's handling of the economy, including about half of young Republicans. Just 9% say the U.S. is headed in the right direction — the lowest in the survey's history. 💸 Topping the list of what young people consider serious threats to their long-term financial security: rising prices and the cost of living (79%) , the power of large corporations (68%) and trade wars (68%) .

 Nearly half said that their household is struggling to make ends meet or is getting by with limited financial security. Data: Harvard Youth Poll; Chart: Emily Peck/Axios Stunning stat: For the first time in the poll's history, the share of young adults who said they expect to be worse off than their parents (27%) slightly exceeded the share who said they expect to be better off (25%) .

 The difference was within the poll's margin of error, but the longer-term trend is striking. In the fall of 2021, 39% expected to be better off than their parents, while just 17% expected to be worse off. Go deeper ... Explore the data . 

 
 
