 2. 👾 Hackers lay dangerous mobile web traps 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Hackers are finding new ways to infect iPhones with spyware even without victims clicking a malicious link, Axios' Sam Sabin reports from new research. 

 🤳 Researchers at iVerify and Report URI say they've uncovered two campaigns in which criminal groups with suspected ties to China exploited online ads and legitimate websites to deliver iPhone spyware.

 In one case, iVerify found that a financial institution employee's iPhone was infected after they scrolled past a pro soccer ad on Instagram. The ad triggered an attack that stole the person's login credentials and searched their cryptocurrency wallets. In another, Report URI uncovered a campaign affecting nearly 8,000 legitimate websites after hackers purchased roughly 630 expired domains still used by those sites. The attackers used those domains to redirect vulnerable iPhone visitors toward spyware designed to steal their credentials and cryptocurrency. ⚠️ Experts advise keeping your phone updated, as the attacks exploited flaws in older software versions.

